1:// gh-1763: bash tool self-signal immunity.
2://
3:// A `bash` tool command running a broad process kill (`pkill -f`,
4:// `killall`, `kill`) used to match its own `setsid sh -c '<command>'`
5:// wrapper (the raw script rode the wrapper argv) and could signal the
6:// harness/ancestor chain. These tests pin the mechanical self-defense:
7://
8:// - AC1: `pkill -f <unique>` inside a bash call cannot kill its own shell
9://   (the wrapper argv never carries the script body).
10:// - AC2: harness/ancestor pids are exported and honored — a protected pid
11://   is skipped by `pkill`/`killall`/`kill` while an unprotected process
12://   matching the same pattern is still reaped.
13:// - E3: `$0`, `$#`/`"$@"`, exit codes and `set -e` behave exactly like the
14://   old `sh -c` delivery.
